Let $\mathcal{M}$ denote a collection of mathematical models of biological systems. Each model $M \in \mathcal{M}$ is specified by a tuple $(F_M, S_M, B_M, P_M)$, where $F_M$ is the mathematical formalism (e.g., ordinary differential equations, partial differential equations, stochastic processes, Boolean networks, agent-based rules, reaction networks, etc.), $S_M$ is the structural description (state variables, interaction topology, governing equations), $B_M$ is a set of boundary and/or initial conditions, and $P_M$ is a parameter regime (a subset of admissible parameter values). For a behavioral phenotype $\Phi$ drawn from a fixed list such as $\{\text{sustained oscillations, perfect/near-perfect adaptation, bistable switching, signal filtering, Turing-type pattern formation}\}$, let $\mathcal{M}_\Phi \subseteq \mathcal{M}$ be the subset of models that exhibit $\Phi$ for some choice of admissible inputs and parameters in $P_M$.

Question: Does there exist a comparison framework — that is, a constructive, formalism-agnostic, and algorithmically implementable procedure $\mathcal{C}$ that assigns to every ordered pair $(M_1, M_2) \in \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{M}$ a structured comparison object $\mathcal{C}(M_1, M_2)$ (for instance, a quantitative similarity score together with a certified correspondence between structural or dynamical components) — such that the following hold:

(i) $\mathcal{C}$ is well-defined regardless of the formalisms $F_{M_1}, F_{M_2}$, the structural descriptions, the boundary conditions, or the parameter regimes of $M_1, M_2$;

(ii) $\mathcal{C}$ is automatable, i.e., computable by an algorithm taking standardized encodings of $M_1, M_2$ as input;

(iii) For each phenotype $\Phi$ in the fixed list, there exists an invariant $I_\Phi$ extractable from the family $\{\mathcal{C}(M_i, M_j) : M_i, M_j \in \mathcal{M}_\Phi\}$ that rigorously characterizes a shared design principle underlying $\Phi$, in the sense that $I_\Phi$ is preserved across all $M \in \mathcal{M}_\Phi$ and discriminates $\mathcal{M}_\Phi$ from $\mathcal{M} \setminus \mathcal{M}_\Phi$?

Construct such a framework $\mathcal{C}$ with invariants $\{I_\Phi\}$, or prove that no such framework can exist.

